I forgot to mention it in the change log above. The rule was set to allow arrow-function component definition without invalidating the already existing function expressions. 

I wanted to propose moving to using arrow functions instead of function expressions for components since they look/feel more convenient and to write functional components, especially typed ones. E.g.:

```tsx
interface ComponentProps {
  prop1: string;
  prop2: number;
  prop3: boolean;
}

const Component: React.FC<ComponentProps> = ({ prop1, prop2, prop3 }) => {
  /* ... */
}
```

Compared to

```tsx
function Component({ prop1, prop2, prop3}: ComponentProps) {
  /* ... */
}
```

It also allows for shorter component definitions if they return only JSX:

```tsx
const Component: React.FC = () => (
  <div>
    /* ... */
  </div>
);
```

Compared to

```tsx
function Component() {
  return (
    <div>
      /* ... */
    </div>
  );
}
```

I suppose that this is also more of an opinionated decision and each developer may have their own preference on how they define components. This is why I set the rule to be `"off"` instead of specifying. At the end of the day, if we follow proper conventions for file-naming, exporting, and folder organization, there should be no confusion on reading and expecting component definitions.

