Prefer using `React.useState` to ensure the component only re-renders when the state changes

```tsx
  // true if any advanced-related setting is already in use
  const [enableAdvanced, setEnableAdvanced] = React.useState(
    !!settings.SECURITY_ANALYZER ||
      !!settings.CONFIRMATION_MODE ||
      (!!settings.LLM_MODEL && !models.includes(settings.LLM_MODEL)),
  );

  React.useEffect(() => {
    setEnableAdvanced(
      !!settings.SECURITY_ANALYZER ||
        !!settings.CONFIRMATION_MODE ||
        (!!settings.LLM_MODEL && !models.includes(settings.LLM_MODEL)),
    );
  }, [settings, models]);
```

> `advancedAlreadyInUse` should only be calculated once

In that case, we would use `React.useMemo` instead:
```tsx
const enableAdvanced = React.useMemo(() => {
    !!settings.SECURITY_ANALYZER ||
      !!settings.CONFIRMATION_MODE ||
      (!!settings.LLM_MODEL && !models.includes(settings.LLM_MODEL)),
}, []);
```

The idea behind React's state management magic is that internally it understands how frequently we want this variable to change. You can set a non-state variable as you did earlier but it would recalculate every time there is a re-render. Wrapping them with a `React.useMemo` for example, tells React that this value should only be calculated once (the empty dependency array as second argument). That way, even if the component or its parent re-renders, the value does not.

